00:00:08.919 --> 00:00:11.810 align:start position:0%
these coffee filters are made of 100%  cellulose fiber harvested from slow  growing pine and spruce trees in the  forests of southern scandinavia 30  percent of the paper cellulose content  is left over from earlier production the  other 70% is raw cellulose fiber the  materials go into a machine called a  popper which works like a giant food  processor a paper making machine spreads  a one-inch layer of Pulp across a wire  mesh belt the pulp forms a soggy sheet  of paper at the next station heavy  compression rollers squeeze almost half  the water out of the paper a tool  applies a crepe texture  next they use 38 steam-heated rollers to  dry the paper completely the roller  surface temperatures are around 250  degrees as the finished paper exits the  machine a camera registers the location  of holes dirt or other defects that have  to be cut out the paper is sliced into 2  foot rolls so it can fit in the filter  making machine sensors in the filter  making machine make sure the paper is  aligned correctly at the machines first  station tiny needles pierce holes in the  paper to enhance the quality of  filtration at the next station a stamp  imprints the company logo in the paper a  knife slices the paper in half a roller  embosses a seam connecting the two  layers of paper along the bottom inside  then a second roller cuts the conical  shape of the filter the machine produces  four thousand filters per minute the  next station cuts the filters apart and  collects the leftover paper which will  be recycled in DePaul  the filters are sent to an automatic  counting machine that divides them into  stacks of 80 the machine feeds the  stacks to the packaging line then it  opens the box with a vacuum and inserts  the filters if the machine sensor  detects a problem like filters in the  wrong position or missing filters it  ejects that box from the line how the  cellulose is processed in the pulper  determines how the filter will perform  the factory processes cellulose finally  to make filters designed to brew strong  coffee fine celos produces paper that  drains slowly keeping the water and  coffee beans together longer resulting  in a stronger flavor
 

